<wpf:ResourceDictionary xml:space="preserve" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml" xmlns:s="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib" xmlns:ss="urn:shemas-jetbrains-com:settings-storage-xaml" xmlns:wpf="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation">

	<!--
	Solution-shared ReSharper settings. Read by both Rider's live inspections and
	`jb inspectcode` in CI, so what you see while editing matches what the build enforces.

	The file is named .sln.DotSettings even though this repo only has RomForge.slnx —
	JetBrains normalises to the .sln name, and inspectcode picks it up regardless.

	Every entry is a deliberate divergence from a ReSharper default, justified in place.
	Do not add blanket suppressions here to silence a real finding.
	-->

	<!--
	Naming is not a CI gate — Rider still surfaces it live, it just does not fail the build.
	ReSharper's defaults disagree with conventions set in /Development/CLAUDE.md (notably
	_camelCase private fields) and with generator-produced members it cannot see the source of.

	Note for anyone tempted to fix this in .editorconfig instead: ReSharper does read
	dotnet_naming_rule, and .editorconfig severity WINS over this file, so adding a rule there
	silently re-enables this inspection.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=InconsistentNaming/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

	<!--
	The pattern flagged is always the same: a `using var` (SemaphoreSlim, HttpClient, temp dir)
	captured by a local function that is only ever run through an `await Task.WhenAll(...)` in
	the same scope. Task.WhenAll completes only once every task has, so disposal cannot race the
	closure. ReSharper cannot prove that lifetime and warns on principle.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=AccessToDisposedClosure/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

	<!--
	CommunityToolkit.Mvvm generates `partial void On{Name}Changed(T oldValue, T newValue)` with
	the property's own nullability, but the backing field starts null — so the first invocation
	passes null for a parameter the generator declared non-nullable. The null guards ReSharper
	calls "always true" are load-bearing; removing one throws on first assignment. This is the
	documented pattern in /Development/CLAUDE.md -> Avalonia UI / CommunityToolkit.Mvvm.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=ConditionIsAlwaysTrueOrFalseAccordingToNullableAPIContract/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

	<!--
	Same generator, other half: the On{Name}Changed / On{Name}Changing hook signatures are fixed
	by CommunityToolkit.Mvvm. A hook that only needs to react, not to read the value, still has
	to declare the parameter. It cannot be removed.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=UnusedParameterInPartialMethod/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

	<!--
	`App` is partial because Avalonia's XAML compiler generates the other half (InitializeComponent
	and the named-control fields) at build time. ReSharper only sees the hand-written part and
	reports a single-part partial; dropping the modifier breaks the build.
	-->
	<s:String x:Key="/Default/CodeInspection/Highlighting/InspectionSeverities/=PartialTypeWithSinglePart/@EntryIndexedValue">DO_NOT_SHOW</s:String>

</wpf:ResourceDictionary>
